## Hypothesis (contract closing paragraph, ≤8 sentences)

In the PCA on the 12 forms-of-capital indicators across the 39 century bins (BCE 4300→500), the three symbolic-capital components — house size (Zhausgr), daggers (ZDolch) and hatchets/swords (ZAxtSchwert) — load on PC1 (30.3% variance, sign aligned so Pottery Form is positive) with **the same sign and similar moderate magnitudes** (−0.23, −0.27, −0.30; 5–9% PC1 contribution each), forming a tight co-varying cluster that distinguishes them jointly from the burial-display/pottery pole on the opposite end of the axis. On PC2 (19.5% variance; cumulative 49.8%) the three indicators are **strongly asymmetric**: house size carries a moderate positive loading (+0.33, ≈11% of PC2 variance) while daggers (+0.07) and hatchets/swords (+0.05) sit essentially **at zero**, so symbolic capital on PC2 is carried almost entirely by house size. Across the 100-yr slices PC2 scores are broadly dispersed — range [−3.70, +2.54] = 6.23 wide, median +0.34, IQR ≈ ±0.99, with 59% of bins (23 of 39) positive — exhibiting a slight positive central tendency and a strong negative outlier tail driven by the three identical deep-prehistoric bins (BCE 4300–4100). Synthesis: **symbolic capital is unified and moderate on PC1, but split on PC2 — house size drives the secondary axis while daggers and hatchets/swords are quasi-orthogonal to it**, so the "distribution along PC2" of symbolic capital effectively reduces to the house-size trajectory, which itself is widely spread but mildly positive across most century slices.

